id: pm-42p9nk
title: "Author-resolution bypass class: create/copy and the context, next, and usage-feedback paths re-implement author precedence without harness detection, stamping author 'unknown' on new items"
description: "The canonical precedence chain lives in core/shared/author.ts (resolveAuthorIdentity: explicit argument, then PM_AUTHOR, then configured default, then detected harness, then unknown). Six presentation-layer sites re-implement it and all of them omit the harness-detection step, so the identity capability that shipped and closed on 2026-07-25 is invisible to them. Two distinct failures result. (1) Attribution: src/cli/commands/create.ts:1352 and src/cli/commands/copy.ts:74 each define a private selectAuthor that resolves explicit ?? PM_AUTHOR ?? settings.author_default and falls back to the literal 'unknown'. With author_default empty - the state of this repository - every item created by a harness-identified agent is stamped author: unknown and writes an unknown-author history event, which is precisely the governance warning that pm-zqpnte just finished dispositioning. Verified on pm-42p9nk, pm-wt43zj, and pm-tuadp2: item author 'unknown' and the create history entry author 'unknown' with author_source 'unknown', while the very next pm update on the same items recorded 'harness:claude-code' with author_source 'detected'. (2) Relevance: src/cli/main.ts:734 (afterCommand context-usage hook), src/cli/commands/context.ts:2597, and src/cli/commands/next.ts:308 and :662 read PM_AUTHOR ?? author_default directly; the empty result makes recordContextUsageTouches throw TypeError('Context usage touch requires author and intent'), the throw is swallowed, and every mutation prints 'afterCommand hook_warnings=context_usage_feedback_write_failed'. Reproduced on every mutation in this session."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[7]: agent-ux,"area:cli","area:sdk",author,context,defect,relevance
created_at: "2026-07-25T06:16:22.783Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-25T06:25:02.551Z"
author: unknown
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "create and copy resolve the acting identity through the canonical resolver instead of a private selectAuthor, so an item created with no PM_AUTHOR and an empty author_default carries the detected harness identity and not the literal 'unknown'; The context-usage hook, pm next, and pm context resolve identity through the same canonical resolver; A workspace with an empty author_default and no PM_AUTHOR records usage touches under the detected identity and emits no hook warning; recordContextUsageTouches raises a typed, named error instead of a bare TypeError, so a swallowed throw can never hide a systemic outage again; A contract test asserts that process.env.PM_AUTHOR is read in exactly one source file and that no other module defines a private author-precedence helper, making the bypass class mechanically impossible to reintroduce; Items created during this session that were stamped 'unknown' are dispositioned through the existing acknowledgment flow rather than by rewriting history"
goal: provable-history
objective: "Author identity is resolved once, by one contract, on every path that needs it"
value: Usage affinity is an input to context relevance ranking; losing it for the default agent workflow silently degrades the quality of every pm context and pm next answer without any visible failure.
impact: "Stops new unknown-author history events from being generated on every create, which would otherwise re-open the governance warning that was just closed; restores the usage-affinity signal for harness-identified agents; and closes the whole duplicated-precedence class with one contract test."
outcome: "One author-resolution contract, no direct PM_AUTHOR reads outside it, no hook warning on the default path, and usage affinity recorded for detected identities."
why_now: "Harness detection shipped and closed on 2026-07-25; the paths that were supposed to consume it were never migrated, so the feature is half-live and its main downstream consumer is dark."
parent: pm-mpbb
risk: medium
confidence: high
